    "q-19157": {
      "dom_id": "step-q19157",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "The table below lists values of $f(x)$ at distinct $x$-values. Knowing that the average rate of change of the function $f(x)$ on the interval $[2p,2]$ is $3,$ find the value of $p.$ \n\n| $x$ | $\\,-9\\,$ | $\\,-4\\,$ | $\\,2p\\,$ | $\\,-1\\,$ | $\\,0\\,$ | $\\,1\\,$ | $\\,2\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\,-12\\,$ | $\\,-8\\,$ | $\\,-4\\,$ | $\\,-3\\,$ | $\\,2\\,$ | $\\,6\\,$ | $\\,8\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "Using the formula for the average rate of change, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(2)-f(2p)}{2-2p} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-(-4)}{2-2p} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{2-2p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=3$ on the interval $[2p,2].$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{2-2p}=3.\n$$\n\nSolving for $p,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12}{2-2p} & =3 \\\\ 12 & =3\\cdot (2-2p) \\\\ 12 & =6-6p \\\\ 12-6 & =-6p \\\\ 6 & =-6p \\\\ \\frac{6}{-6} & =\\frac{-6p}{-6} \\\\ -1 & =p.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $p=-1.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
