    "q-21129": {
      "dom_id": "step-q21129",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "The ice level in the North Pole is decreasing due to global warming. The thickness of ice, measured in feet, in the North Pole, is represented by the expression $135+0.1x-0.02{x}^{2},$ where $x$ is the time, in years, since measurements began. What is the average rate of decrease of the ice thickness over the first $10$ years?",
      "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)=135+0.1x-0.02{x}^{2}$ be the thickness of the ice, in feet, after $x$ years. To find the average rate of change of the ice thickness over the first $10$ years, we use the average rate of change formula on the interval $[0,10]$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(10)-f(0)}{10-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{[135+0.1(10)-0.02(10)^{2}]-[135+0.1(0)-0.02(0)^{2}]}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{134-135}{10} \\\\  & =-0.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average rate of decrease of the ice thickness over the first $10$ years is $0.1$ feet per year.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
